## [:< ##

# name  = user-edit.form.add_field_name
# descr = the add-a-field row's internal field name

## ONE constant, not two hardcoded copies -- user-edit.form.add_field_row
## needs it for the real schema field it creates, and user-edit.form.
## build_frame needs the IDENTICAL string for its width RESERVATION of that
## same row before it exists.  a second literal copy would drift exactly the
## way editor.control.list.summary's own header warns against.
##
## PADDED, not the bare 'add_field' this started as.  read on its own the
## short form was still perfectly valid [ ^\w+$, leading underscore reserved
## so it cannot collide with a real record field ] -- the padding exists
## purely to widen the MOCKUP PLACEHOLDER TOKEN [ '{{name}}' ], which
## user-edit.form.build_frame's required_width_for identity treats as the
## row's ASSUMED content size.  a short name meant the row's REAL rendered
## text [ the whole cycler, routinely 30-45+ columns ] overshot that
## assumption by just as much, and that overshoot gets ADDED ON TOP OF
## whatever the label column already needs -- not maxed with it, since every
## row shares one frame width.  padded to 30 characters : close to the average
## cycler width across a 2-4 choice steady state [ measured against this
## vocabulary's own name lengths : 24 / 35 / 46 columns for 2 / 3 / 4
## remaining choices ], so the row's assumed size and its real size are close
## enough that the compounding mostly disappears without ever under-reserving
## [ a real value SHORTER than the padded token just leaves unused suffix,
## which is harmless -- only a LONGER real value than assumed re-triggers the
## overshoot, at the full 6-choice extreme this does not eliminate ]

return qw| _add_field____________________ |;

